{
  "chapter": "human-in-the-loop/approval-gates",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "Three decisions make a gate: where it sits, what it shows, and what answers it accepts.",
    "Most teams decide only the first.",
    "The gate node holds exactly one side-effecting call, because a resume re-executes it from",
    "the top and anything else in there fires twice.",
    "A reviewer needs the exact action, the changed state, the authority, the evidence, the",
    "uncertainty, the alternatives and the limits on reversal. Hide one and you have added",
    "latency without oversight.",
    "Four answers, not two. Edit is the one reviewers actually want, and its absence forces",
    "real corrections into denials.",
    "The deny path is a branch rather than an end state, and the reason should be written as an",
    "instruction the model can act on.",
    "Approvals expire, because an approval is a decision about a state and the state moves. Set",
    "expiry from data volatility and enforce it in a validator.",
    "Use an update rather than a signal, so staleness can be rejected before it is recorded."
  ],
  "policy": {
    "required": [
      "action",
      "changedState",
      "authority",
      "evidence",
      "uncertainty",
      "alternatives",
      "reversal"
    ],
    "laneBudgetMs": {
      "fast": 60000,
      "deliberate": 3600000
    },
    "volatilityMs": 900000
  },
